Market Challenges
Technical Difficulties in Large-Area Wafer Production
Producing larger CdZnTe wafers while maintaining crystal uniformity and low defect levels remains difficult. Crystal growth methods require precise process control, and manufacturing yields can be affected by material imperfections. These limitations restrict scalability and make it challenging to serve applications that require large-area detector substrates.

Competition from Alternative Semiconductor Materials
Silicon, silicon carbide, and emerging semiconductor materials compete with CdZnTe across selected sensing and electronic applications. Alternative materials may offer advantages in cost, manufacturing scalability, or environmental characteristics. CdZnTe suppliers must continue improving performance and production efficiency to maintain their position in specialized detection markets.

Market Restraints
High Production Costs and Raw Material Constraints
CdZnTe wafer manufacturing involves specialized crystal growth, processing, and quality-control procedures. The report identifies production costs for high-quality single-crystal wafers as substantially higher than those of competing semiconductor materials. Tellurium supply constraints can also affect material availability and manufacturing economics.

Environmental Regulations on Cadmium
Cadmium is a toxic heavy metal, and its use is subject to environmental and regulatory scrutiny. Manufacturers must account for applicable handling, processing, and disposal requirements. Compliance obligations can increase operating costs and create uncertainty around capacity expansion and applications in more tightly regulated industries.

